// cs-strategy — generated from src/cs-strategy-*.js by tools/merge.js. Edit the src files, then run: node tools/merge.js
// Strategy / Business Analyst. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["cs-strategy"] = (QB["cs-strategy"] || []).concat([
["Role", "What does a strategy or business analyst do?", "**Why they ask:** the opening question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I help the organization make better decisions by analyzing problems and opportunities — gathering and analyzing data, building financial and business models, sizing markets, evaluating options, and turning analysis into clear recommendations for leadership. I break ambiguous business questions into structured analysis and tell a clear story with the findings. The value isn't the spreadsheet — it's the insight and the sound recommendation that helps leaders decide and act.\"\n\nTip: Turn ambiguous questions into structured analysis and clear recommendations.", "B", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's the real deliverable?","Not the analysis itself but the insight and recommendation that helps leadership make a better decision and act."]}],
["Problem solving", "How do you approach an ambiguous business problem?", "**Why they ask:** the core skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I structure it — clarify the real question, break it into components (a logic tree or framework), form hypotheses about the likely drivers, then gather data to test them, prioritizing the analysis that matters most. I avoid boiling the ocean; I focus on the few things that drive the answer. Then I synthesize into a clear recommendation. Structuring the problem well is most of the battle — it turns an overwhelming question into answerable pieces.\"\n\nTip: Structure, hypothesize, prioritize the analysis that drives the answer.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What does 'don't boil the ocean' mean?","Don't analyze everything exhaustively — focus on the few drivers that actually determine the answer, to be fast and sharp."]}],
["Frameworks", "What is MECE and why is it useful?", "**Why they ask:** foundational structuring concept.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"MECE means Mutually Exclusive, Collectively Exhaustive — breaking a problem into parts that don't overlap and together cover everything. It keeps analysis organized, avoids double-counting and gaps, and makes the logic clear and defensible. For example, breaking profit into revenue and cost, then each of those into their drivers. Structuring problems MECE is a core consulting skill because it forces complete, non-overlapping thinking.\"\n\nTip: Mutually Exclusive, Collectively Exhaustive — complete, non-overlapping.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Give a MECE breakdown of profit.","Profit = Revenue − Cost; Revenue = price × volume; Cost = fixed + variable — each level complete and non-overlapping."]}],
["Market sizing", "How would you size a market?", "**Why they ask:** a classic analytical test.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd use a structured, top-down or bottom-up approach with clear assumptions. Bottom-up: estimate the number of potential customers, the amount each would buy, and the price, then multiply — stating each assumption so it's transparent and can be refined. I sanity-check against a top-down estimate. The point isn't a perfect number but a defensible estimate with explicit, reasonable assumptions that can be pressure-tested. Showing clear logic matters more than precision.\"\n\nTip: Structured estimate with explicit, defensible assumptions; cross-check.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Top-down vs bottom-up sizing?","Top-down starts from a big number and narrows with percentages; bottom-up builds up from units × price. Cross-check both."]}],
["Analysis", "How do you build a business case or financial model?", "**Why they ask:** core deliverable.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I define the decision and key drivers, build a model with transparent assumptions, project costs and benefits, calculate metrics like ROI, NPV or payback, and test sensitivity to the key assumptions so leadership sees the range and risks. I make assumptions explicit and the model easy to follow and audit. A business case is only as good as its assumptions, so I stress-test them and present honest downside scenarios, not just the rosy one.\"\n\nTip: Transparent assumptions, key metrics, sensitivity analysis.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why do sensitivity analysis?","To show how the result changes if key assumptions are wrong — revealing the risks and which assumptions matter most."]}],
["Data", "How do you turn data into insight?", "**Why they ask:** analysis must mean something.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start from the question, analyze the data to find the patterns and drivers that answer it, and ask 'so what does this mean and what should we do'. Data alone isn't insight — a chart of numbers isn't a recommendation. I distill findings into the key message, support it with the evidence, and connect it to a decision. The skill is separating the signal that matters from the noise and making it actionable.\"\n\nTip: Data → 'so what' → actionable recommendation.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's the difference between data and insight?","Data is numbers; insight is what they mean and what to do about it — the 'so what' that drives a decision."]}],
["Communication", "How do you present findings to executives?", "**Why they ask:** communication is half the job.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Lead with the answer — the recommendation and key message first (top-down / answer-first), then the supporting logic and evidence, concisely. Executives have little time and want the 'so what', not a journey through all my analysis. I use clear visuals, anticipate their questions, and am honest about assumptions and risks. The best analysis fails if it's not communicated clearly, so I tell a crisp, structured story that drives the decision.\"\n\nTip: Answer-first, concise, anticipate questions.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's 'answer-first' communication?","Stating the conclusion and recommendation up front, then the supporting reasoning — not building up to it slowly."]}],
["Judgment", "How do you make a recommendation with incomplete data?", "**Why they ask:** real-world reality.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I rarely have perfect data, so I use the best available, make reasonable assumptions explicit, and apply judgment — a timely, well-reasoned recommendation beats a perfect one that comes too late. I'm clear about the confidence level and what would change my conclusion, and I note what further analysis would reduce the uncertainty if it matters. Decisiveness with transparency about assumptions is what leaders need.\"\n\nTip: Best available data + explicit assumptions + judgment; be clear on confidence.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why not wait for complete data?","Decisions have timing; a good-enough answer now usually beats a perfect one too late, as long as the risks are clear."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about an analysis that changed a decision.", "**Why they ask:** real impact.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"Leadership assumed a product line was unprofitable and wanted to cut it. I analyzed it properly — allocating costs correctly and looking at contribution margin — and found it was actually contributing positively once shared costs were handled right; the real problem was a different line. My analysis reversed the decision and redirected attention to the actual issue, saving a profitable product.\"\n\nTip: Show analysis that overturned a wrong assumption.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What was the analytical key?","Correct cost allocation and looking at contribution margin, rather than the misleading fully-loaded view that made it look unprofitable."]}],
["Stakeholders", "How do you handle stakeholders who disagree with your analysis?", "**Why they ask:** influence and rigor.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I listen to understand their concern — they may know something I don't, or my assumptions may need revisiting. I walk through my logic and data transparently, address their specific objections, and update if they're right. If my analysis holds, I defend it respectfully with evidence. The goal is the right answer, not winning — being open to challenge while standing on sound analysis builds credibility.\"\n\nTip: Listen, be transparent, update if warranted, defend with evidence.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What if a senior leader just doesn't like the answer?","Present the evidence objectively and respectfully; my job is to give the honest analysis, even when it's unwelcome."]}],
["Closing", "Why strategy and analysis?", "**Why they ask:** motivation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I love solving ambiguous, high-stakes problems with structured thinking and data, and seeing my analysis actually shape decisions. It's intellectually demanding, varied, and impactful, and it combines analytical rigor with communication and business judgment — the mix I'm strongest in and enjoy most.\"\n\nTip: Structured problem-solving with real decision impact.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's your strongest analytical skill?","Pick one — structuring problems, modeling, or communicating insight — with an example."]}]
]);

// Strategy / Business Analyst — part 2. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["cs-strategy"] = (QB["cs-strategy"] || []).concat([
["Method", "What does a hypothesis-driven approach mean and why use one?", "**Why they ask:** it's the backbone of efficient problem-solving in strategy work.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Instead of boiling the ocean, I start with a best-guess answer — a hypothesis — and then design analysis to confirm or kill it. If I think a client's margin problem is driven by one product line, I go straight to the data that would prove or disprove that, rather than analyzing everything equally. It focuses effort on what matters and gets to an answer faster. The discipline is holding the hypothesis loosely: I'm testing it to be right about the problem, not to defend my first guess, so I update fast when the data disagrees.\"\n\nTip: Start with a testable hypothesis, analyze to confirm or kill it fast — and hold it loosely so data can overturn it.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's the risk of being hypothesis-driven?","Confirmation bias — getting attached to the initial guess; you counter it by actively seeking disconfirming evidence, not just support."]}],
["Method", "What is an issue tree and how do you use it?", "**Why they ask:** issue trees are a core structuring tool.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"An issue tree breaks a big question into MECE sub-questions, then those into smaller ones, until you reach pieces you can actually analyze. For 'how do we raise profit,' the first split is revenue versus cost; revenue splits into price and volume; volume into customers and purchase frequency, and so on. It turns a vague problem into a map of specific, answerable questions, shows where to dig, and makes sure nothing's missed or double-counted. I also use it to prioritize — I don't analyze every branch equally, I chase the branches most likely to hold the answer.\"\n\nTip: Decompose the question into MECE branches down to analyzable pieces — then prioritize the branches most likely to hold the answer.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How does an issue tree connect to prioritization?","Once the problem is mapped into branches, you focus analysis on the few most likely to drive the answer rather than working every branch equally."]}],
["Prioritization", "How do you apply the 80/20 principle in analysis?", "**Why they ask:** prioritization under time pressure is essential.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Most of the impact usually sits in a small share of the drivers, so I find that share first. If I'm analyzing cost, I'll quickly see which few categories make up the bulk of spend and focus there, rather than spending equal time on a line item that's two percent of cost. It's about getting 80 percent of the insight for 20 percent of the effort early, so the recommendation is directionally right fast, then refining only where precision changes the decision. The failure mode is perfecting an analysis of something that doesn't move the needle.\"\n\nTip: Find the few drivers holding most of the impact, get directionally right fast, and refine only where precision changes the decision.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["When is more precision actually worth it?","When the extra accuracy would change the recommendation or the decision — otherwise it's polishing a number that doesn't matter."]}],
["Framework", "How would you diagnose why a company's profits are declining?", "**Why they ask:** the classic profitability case tests structured thinking.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Profit is revenue minus cost, so I split it. On revenue: is it price or volume, and which product, segment, or region — is the whole market shrinking or are we losing share? On cost: fixed or variable, which categories rose, and why. I'd quantify each branch to find where the decline actually lives — say volume is flat but a key product's margin collapsed on input costs. Then I'd ask why that's happening — competition, mix shift, a cost shock — before recommending. The structure keeps me from jumping to a fix before I know the cause.\"\n\nTip: Split profit into revenue (price x volume, by segment) and cost (fixed/variable), quantify each branch to locate the cause before fixing.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why separate an industry-wide decline from a company-specific one?","The fix is completely different — a shrinking market calls for repositioning or exit, while losing share to rivals calls for competitive response."]}],
["Framework", "When is Porter's Five Forces useful, and when is it not?", "**Why they ask:** tests whether you use frameworks thoughtfully, not reflexively.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Five Forces — rivalry, new entrants, substitutes, buyer power, supplier power — is useful for assessing the structural attractiveness and profit potential of an industry, like whether to enter a market or why margins are what they are. It's less useful for fast-moving or platform markets where the boundaries blur, and it's a snapshot, not dynamic. I treat it as one lens to make sure I've considered the structural pressures, not a template to fill in mechanically. A framework earns its place only if it sharpens the specific question; otherwise it's just scaffolding on the slide.\"\n\nTip: Great for industry structure and entry decisions; weak for dynamic/platform markets — use it as a lens, not a fill-in-the-blank template.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the danger of applying frameworks mechanically?","You produce generic analysis that fits any company and answers nothing — the framework should sharpen the specific question or be dropped."]}],
["Case", "How would you approach a market-entry decision?", "**Why they ask:** market entry is a staple strategy case.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd structure it around a few questions: is the market attractive — size, growth, profitability, competition; can we win — do we have a right to play and a differentiated advantage; and does it make financial sense — the investment, the returns, the risks and how we'd enter (build, buy, or partner). Each gets real analysis, not a gut call. The entry only makes sense if the market's attractive, we can actually win a worthwhile position, and the economics clear our hurdle. I'd also stress-test the downside and define what we'd need to believe for it to work.\"\n\nTip: Is the market attractive, can we win, and do the economics work — plus the entry mode and the downside case.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why isn't an attractive market enough on its own?","If you can't win a defensible, profitable position — no advantage, entrenched incumbents — an attractive market just means strong competition you'll lose to."]}],
["Case", "How do you think about a pricing decision for a product?", "**Why they ask:** pricing is high-leverage and frequently tested.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Three lenses: cost (the floor — you won't sustainably sell below it), competition (what rivals and substitutes charge), and value (what the product is worth to the customer, the real ceiling). Cost-plus alone leaves money on the table or prices you out; the strongest approach anchors on value — quantifying the benefit to the customer versus their alternative — then checks it against competition and cost. I'd also consider willingness-to-pay across segments, since one price rarely fits all, and the strategic goal: are we maximizing margin, gaining share, or signaling quality?\"\n\nTip: Price on value (ceiling), bounded by cost (floor) and competition — and segment by willingness to pay.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is value-based pricing usually stronger than cost-plus?","Cost-plus ignores what the customer will actually pay, so it either underprices high-value offerings or overprices low-value ones — value anchors to the real ceiling."]}],
["Finance", "Walk me through a break-even analysis with an example.", "**Why they ask:** a basic quantitative skill a business analyst must have.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Break-even is the volume where total revenue equals total cost, so profit is zero. The formula is fixed costs divided by the contribution margin per unit — price minus variable cost. For example, if fixed costs are 100,000, the price is 50, and variable cost is 30, contribution is 20 per unit, so break-even is 100,000 divided by 20, which is 5,000 units. Below that you lose money; above it each unit adds 20 of profit. It's useful for go/no-go decisions, pricing, and understanding how sensitive profit is to volume — and it immediately shows how a price or cost change shifts the required volume.\"\n\nTip: Break-even = fixed cost / (price - variable cost); e.g., 100,000 / (50-30) = 5,000 units.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What happens to break-even if you cut price from 50 to 45?","Contribution falls to 15, so break-even rises to about 6,667 units — a price cut needs meaningfully more volume to stay profitable."]}],
["Finance", "Explain NPV and why it matters for a business case.", "**Why they ask:** investment decisions hinge on discounted cash flow.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Net present value sums a project's future cash flows discounted back to today at a rate reflecting the cost of capital and risk, minus the initial investment. It matters because a dollar next year is worth less than a dollar today, so comparing raw future cash to upfront cost overstates returns. If NPV is positive, the project creates value above its cost of capital; negative, it destroys value. For example, 110,000 received in a year, discounted at 10 percent, is worth 100,000 today — so paying more than 100,000 for it would be NPV-negative. I pair NPV with payback and a sensitivity on the key assumptions.\"\n\nTip: NPV discounts future cash flows to today minus investment; positive means value created above the cost of capital.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why not just use payback period instead?","Payback ignores the time value of money and everything after the payback point — NPV captures the full value and risk-adjusted return."]}],
["Analysis", "How do you approach a cost-reduction engagement responsibly?", "**Why they ask:** cost cutting is common and easy to do badly.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start by understanding the cost base — where the money actually goes — and separate value-adding spend from waste, because the goal is efficiency, not just cutting. I prioritize by size and ease: the big categories with clear waste first. I'm careful about cuts that save now but cost later — gutting R&D, maintenance, or customer service can hit revenue and morale harder than the savings. So I distinguish structural cost reduction from short-term slash-and-burn, quantify both the savings and the risks, and recommend cuts the business can sustain without hollowing out its ability to compete.\"\n\nTip: Understand the cost base, cut waste not capability, prioritize big clear wins, and weigh downstream revenue and morale risks.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the danger of across-the-board cuts?","They hit healthy and wasteful spend equally, often damaging revenue-driving or essential functions — targeted cuts preserve the capability to compete."]}],
["Finance", "What are unit economics, and how do you use CAC and LTV?", "**Why they ask:** unit economics is central to evaluating a business model.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Unit economics is the profit of a single customer or unit, stripped of overhead. For subscription or recurring businesses the two key numbers are customer acquisition cost — what it costs to win a customer — and lifetime value, the margin that customer generates over their relationship. The health check is the LTV-to-CAC ratio: if it costs 300 to acquire a customer worth 1,200 in lifetime margin, that's a 4-to-1 ratio, generally healthy, and I'd also look at payback period. If CAC exceeds LTV, growth just loses money faster, so the model doesn't work no matter how fast it scales.\"\n\nTip: LTV/CAC shows whether each customer pays back acquisition cost; a 3-4x ratio and short payback signal a healthy model.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why can a fast-growing company still be unhealthy?","If CAC exceeds LTV, every new customer loses money — scaling just accelerates the losses rather than fixing them."]}],
["Case", "How do you evaluate a build-versus-buy-versus-partner decision?", "**Why they ask:** a common strategic choice for capabilities and growth.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I weigh speed, cost, control, and risk. Building gives the most control and fit but is slowest and riskiest if it's outside the core competence. Buying is faster to capability but costs capital and carries integration risk. Partnering is fastest and lightest but cedes control and depends on the partner's incentives. The decision turns on how strategic and differentiating the capability is — if it's core to competitive advantage, you usually build or buy to own it; if it's important but not differentiating, partner. I'd quantify the cost and time of each path and the strategic value of owning it.\"\n\nTip: Trade off speed, cost, control, and risk — own (build/buy) what's core and differentiating, partner for important-but-not-differentiating.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why own a capability that's core to your advantage?","If it's what differentiates you, depending on a partner cedes control of your edge and lets them capture or copy the value — owning protects the moat."]}],
["Analysis", "What is customer segmentation and why does it matter for strategy?", "**Why they ask:** segmentation underpins targeting, pricing, and product decisions.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Segmentation groups customers by meaningful differences — needs, behavior, value, or willingness to pay — so the business can serve each group with the right product, price, and channel instead of treating everyone the same. It matters because averages hide the action: a company might find a small segment drives most of the profit, or that a 'growth' segment is actually unprofitable. Good segments are distinct, sizable, and reachable, and they map to a different action. I use it to focus investment on the segments worth winning and to avoid one-size-fits-all strategies that fit no one well.\"\n\nTip: Group customers by meaningful, actionable differences — good segments are distinct, sizable, reachable, and each maps to a different action.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What makes a segmentation useful versus just interesting?","Each segment must imply a different action — different product, price, or channel; if the groups don't change what you'd do, the segmentation is decorative."]}],
["Communication", "What is the 'so what' and why do you insist on it in analysis?", "**Why they ask:** turning data into decisions is the whole value of a strategist.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The 'so what' is the implication — what the finding means for the decision, not just the fact. 'Sales fell 10 percent' is data; 'sales fell 10 percent because we lost our two largest accounts to a cheaper competitor, so we need a retention response now' is a so-what that drives action. I push every analysis to its so-what because executives don't need my charts, they need the decision the charts point to. If I can't state the implication, I haven't finished the analysis — I've just gathered facts.\"\n\nTip: Always state the implication for the decision, not just the finding — 'what it means and what to do,' not 'what happened.'", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you know when an analysis is finished?","When it yields a clear so-what — an implication that changes or confirms a decision; raw facts without an implication mean the work isn't done."]}],
["Communication", "What is the pyramid principle or storylining, and how do you use it?", "**Why they ask:** structured communication is a core consulting skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The pyramid principle structures communication answer-first: lead with the main recommendation, then the few supporting arguments, then the evidence beneath each. So a deck or memo starts with 'we should enter this market,' supported by three reasons, each backed by analysis — rather than making the reader wade through analysis to reach the point. Executives are busy and want the answer and the logic, not a mystery novel. I storyline the whole document this way before building slides, so the argument is sound and tight first, and the slides just serve the story rather than becoming the story.\"\n\nTip: Lead with the answer, then grouped supporting arguments, then evidence — storyline the logic before building slides.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why storyline before building slides?","It forces the argument to be logically sound and complete first, so you don't polish slides for a story that doesn't actually hold together."]}],
["Analysis", "How do you do a top-down versus bottom-up estimate, and why do both?", "**Why they ask:** triangulation is a hallmark of rigorous estimation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Top-down starts from a big aggregate and narrows — total population times penetration times spend. Bottom-up builds from the units up — number of outlets times customers each times purchase size. Doing both and checking whether they roughly agree is the real power: if top-down says a market is 2 billion and bottom-up says 500 million, one of my assumptions is badly off and I go find which. Triangulating two independent paths catches errors a single estimate hides and gives a defensible range rather than one fragile number.\"\n\nTip: Build the estimate two independent ways (top-down and bottom-up) and reconcile them — a big gap flags a wrong assumption to chase.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What do you do when the two estimates diverge widely?","Treat the gap as a signal, trace which assumptions drive the difference, and correct the flawed one — the divergence is information, not a nuisance."]}],
["Modeling", "What makes a good financial or Excel model?", "**Why they ask:** analysts live in models and bad ones cause bad decisions.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A good model is transparent, auditable, and easy to flex. I separate inputs, calculations, and outputs clearly; label assumptions so anyone can see and change them; avoid hardcoding numbers inside formulas; and keep the logic traceable rather than a maze of nested cells. It should be driven by a handful of clear assumptions I can run sensitivities on, so decision-makers see how the answer moves with the key drivers. A model nobody but me can understand or check is a liability — the point is a tool others can trust and interrogate, not a black box.\"\n\nTip: Separate inputs/calcs/outputs, label and avoid hardcoding assumptions, keep logic traceable and easy to flex for sensitivities.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why avoid hardcoding numbers inside formulas?","Hidden constants make a model wrong and unauditable when assumptions change — keeping assumptions as visible, labeled inputs lets anyone check and update them."]}],
["Modeling", "How and why do you run sensitivity analysis on a recommendation?", "**Why they ask:** decisions rest on uncertain assumptions.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I flex the key assumptions to see how much the conclusion depends on each. If the recommendation flips when growth is 8 percent instead of 10, that assumption is load-bearing and I need to be confident in it or hedge for it; if the answer holds across a wide range, I can be bolder. I also build a downside case, not just the base, so leadership sees the risk, not a single false-precision number. The honest version of a recommendation says 'this holds unless these one or two things go badly, and here's what we'd do then.'\"\n\nTip: Flex key assumptions to find which ones the answer depends on; present a downside case, not a single point estimate.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What does it mean if the recommendation is very sensitive to one assumption?","That assumption is the real decision — you either get confident in it, pilot to test it, or build a hedge, rather than betting on a single uncertain number."]}],
["Evidence", "How do you weigh qualitative evidence against quantitative data?", "**Why they ask:** good strategists use both and know each one's limits.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Numbers tell you what's happening; qualitative evidence — customer interviews, frontline input, expert views — often tells you why, and flags things not yet in the data. I use data as the backbone but treat a strong qualitative signal as a prompt to look harder, not as noise to dismiss. When they conflict, I dig in rather than defaulting to whichever is more convenient: sometimes the data is lagging or measuring the wrong thing, sometimes the anecdote is an outlier. The best insights usually come from the two together — a pattern in the numbers explained by what people on the ground are saying.\"\n\nTip: Data shows what, qualitative shows why and what's not yet in the data — use both, and dig in when they conflict rather than dismissing one.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What do you do when data and field input conflict?","Investigate rather than pick the convenient one — the data may lag or mismeasure, or the anecdote may be an outlier; reconciling them usually reveals the real story."]}]
]);

// Strategy / Business Analyst — part 3. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["cs-strategy"] = (QB["cs-strategy"] || []).concat([
["Prioritization", "How do you prioritize a long list of initiatives for a client?", "**Why they ask:** strategy work produces more ideas than anyone can execute.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I score them on impact versus effort or feasibility — a simple two-by-two surfaces the quick wins (high impact, low effort) to do now, the big bets (high impact, high effort) to plan and resource, and the low-impact items to drop or defer. But I don't stop at the grid: I check dependencies, since some initiatives unlock others, and capacity, since a client can only execute so much at once. The real value is giving leadership a sequenced, realistic roadmap — a focused few that will actually happen — rather than a wish list of twenty things that overwhelms the organization.\"\n\nTip: Score on impact vs effort, respect dependencies and capacity, and deliver a sequenced, realistic few — not an overwhelming wish list.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why limit the number of prioritized initiatives?","Organizations have finite execution capacity; twenty priorities means no priorities — a focused few actually get done and show results."]}],
["Analysis", "How do you use benchmarking effectively?", "**Why they ask:** benchmarking is common and often misused.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Benchmarking compares the client's performance or practices against peers or best-in-class to spot gaps and set targets — if rivals convert at twice our rate, that's a quantified opportunity. But I use it carefully: a benchmark tells you what, not why, and blindly copying a leader's practice ignores context and your own strategy. So I benchmark to size gaps and provoke the right questions, then do the diagnostic to understand whether the gap is closeable and how. The failure mode is treating 'best practice' as a recipe rather than as a prompt to understand what actually drives the difference.\"\n\nTip: Benchmark to size gaps and provoke questions, not to copy practices blindly — context and the underlying driver matter.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why not just copy the best-in-class performer's practices?","Their practices fit their context and strategy; copied blindly they may not transfer — benchmark to find the gap, then diagnose what's really driving it."]}],
["Planning", "What is scenario planning and when is it the right tool?", "**Why they ask:** strategy under deep uncertainty needs scenarios, not point forecasts.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Scenario planning builds a few distinct, plausible futures around the big uncertainties — say, how fast a technology adopts and how regulation moves — and tests the strategy against each. It's the right tool when the future is genuinely uncertain in ways a single forecast would falsely smooth over, like entering an emerging or heavily disrupted market. The value isn't predicting which scenario happens; it's finding strategies robust across several, spotting early-warning signals, and pre-deciding responses so the organization isn't caught flat-footed. It complements, rather than replaces, the base-case financial model.\"\n\nTip: Build a few plausible futures around the key uncertainties and find strategies robust across them — it's about preparedness, not prediction.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How is scenario planning different from sensitivity analysis?","Sensitivity flexes one assumption in a model; scenario planning builds whole coherent alternative futures across several interacting uncertainties to test strategic robustness."]}],
["Metrics", "How do you decide which KPIs a business should track?", "**Why they ask:** the wrong metrics drive the wrong behavior.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I pick metrics that tie directly to the strategy and that people can actually influence, and I keep the set small so focus isn't diluted. I look for leading indicators, not just lagging ones — pipeline and customer satisfaction predict future revenue, where revenue itself only reports the past. And I watch for metrics that can be gamed or that drive perverse behavior: a call-center measured only on call time will rush customers. The test is whether moving the metric genuinely means the business is healthier, and whether the people measured can meaningfully affect it.\"\n\nTip: Few metrics, tied to strategy, leading not just lagging, hard to game — and within people's control to influence.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Give an example of a metric that drives bad behavior.","Measuring a call center only on handle time pushes agents to rush customers off the phone, hurting satisfaction and first-call resolution."]}],
["Case", "How do you analyze the synergies in a potential acquisition?", "**Why they ask:** M&A cases test rigor and skepticism about deal hype.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I separate cost synergies — overlapping functions, procurement scale, facility consolidation, which are usually more reliable — from revenue synergies like cross-selling, which are real but routinely overestimated. I'd quantify each bottom-up, discount them for the risk and time to achieve, and net out the integration costs and dis-synergies, like lost customers or departed talent, that deals tend to ignore. Then I compare the risk-adjusted synergy value to the premium being paid: a deal only creates value if the achievable synergies exceed the premium. My job is the honest number, which is often below the pitch.\"\n\nTip: Quantify cost vs revenue synergies separately, discount revenue synergies heavily, net integration costs, and compare to the premium paid.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why discount revenue synergies more than cost synergies?","Cross-sell and growth synergies depend on customer behavior and execution and are routinely overstated, while cost synergies are more within the company's control."]}],
["Case", "How do you think about organic versus inorganic growth?", "**Why they ask:** growth strategy is a core topic.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Organic growth — selling more through existing or new products and markets you build yourself — is lower risk, preserves culture, and compounds, but it's slower. Inorganic growth through acquisition or partnership buys scale, capability, or market access fast, but costs capital and carries integration and overpayment risk. The right mix depends on the gap and the clock: if a capability is core and there's time, build it; if speed or a hard-to-replicate asset matters, buy. I'd map the growth targets to the fastest credible path for each and be honest that acquisitions often disappoint when they're used to paper over a weak organic engine.\"\n\nTip: Organic is lower-risk and compounding but slow; inorganic is fast but costly and risky — match each growth gap to the fastest credible path.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["When is acquiring growth a warning sign?","When it's used to mask a weak organic business — buying revenue to hide an inability to grow the core usually destroys value."]}],
["Framework", "Is SWOT analysis useful, and how do you avoid its pitfalls?", "**Why they ask:** SWOT is ubiquitous and often shallow.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"SWOT can be a useful quick organizing check — strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, threats — early in understanding a situation. Its pitfall is staying at the level of a generic list that doesn't lead anywhere: 'strong brand, strong competition' tells no one what to do. To make it useful I force it to be specific and relative to competitors, prioritize the few items that actually matter, and crucially connect it to action — using strengths to seize which opportunity, fixing which weakness against which threat. A SWOT that doesn't drive a decision is just a brainstorm on a slide.\"\n\nTip: Keep it specific and relative to competitors, prioritize what matters, and connect each item to an action — otherwise it's a generic list.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How do you make a SWOT actionable?","Connect quadrants: use which strength to capture which opportunity, shore up which weakness against which threat — turn the list into specific moves."]}],
["Research", "What's the difference between primary and secondary research, and when do you use each?", "**Why they ask:** gathering evidence efficiently matters.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Secondary research uses existing sources — industry reports, filings, published data — fast and cheap, and I always start there to get the lay of the land and avoid reinventing known facts. Primary research is new data I gather myself — customer interviews, surveys, expert calls, store visits — which I turn to when the question is specific to the client and the answer isn't already out there. The skill is sequencing: exhaust cheap secondary sources first to sharpen the questions, then spend primary-research effort only on the gaps that actually drive the decision.\"\n\nTip: Start with fast, cheap secondary sources to frame the problem; use primary research only for the decision-driving gaps it can't answer.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why start with secondary research?","It's fast and cheap, prevents reinventing known facts, and sharpens the specific questions worth spending costly primary research on."]}],
["Client", "How do you build credibility with a client team that's skeptical of consultants?", "**Why they ask:** influence depends on trust, which isn't automatic.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I earn it rather than assume it. I listen first and show genuine respect for their knowledge of their own business, because they've often seen outside advisors parachute in with generic answers. I'm quick to admit what I don't yet know, back my points with their own data, and deliver a few small useful insights early rather than disappearing for weeks to produce a grand reveal. I also make them partners in the analysis so the recommendation is co-owned, not imposed. Credibility comes from being useful, honest, and respectful of their expertise — not from the logo or the framework.\"\n\nTip: Listen first, respect their expertise, admit what you don't know, deliver early useful insight, and co-own the work — credibility is earned, not assumed.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why co-create the recommendation with the client?","A recommendation the client helped build is one they own and will actually implement, versus an outside answer they resist or quietly ignore."]}],
["Implementation", "A great recommendation won't matter if it isn't implemented. How do you set it up to stick?", "**Why they ask:** strategy that dies on a slide is worthless.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I design for implementation from the start, not as an afterthought. That means involving the people who'll execute it so they buy in and the plan is realistic; translating the strategy into a concrete roadmap with owners, milestones, and resources; and being honest about the change-management challenge — what has to change in behavior, incentives, or capability for it to actually happen. I'd rather recommend something slightly less ambitious that the organization will truly do than a brilliant plan it can't absorb. The measure of a strategy is the change it produces, not the elegance of the deck.\"\n\nTip: Design for execution from the start — involve the doers, give owners and milestones, and address the real change-management barriers.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why might a less ambitious plan be the better recommendation?","Because a plan the organization can actually absorb and execute delivers real change, while a brilliant one it can't implement delivers nothing."]}],
["Teamwork", "How do you work effectively on a lean team under tight deadlines?", "**Why they ask:** consulting and strategy work is intense and collaborative.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I get aligned fast on what the answer needs to be and who owns which workstream, so we're not duplicating or leaving gaps. I communicate progress and blockers early rather than going quiet and surprising the team at the deadline, and I ask for help before I'm stuck for hours. Under time pressure I ruthlessly prioritize the analysis that drives the answer and resist perfecting things that don't. And I pitch in beyond my piece when a teammate is underwater, because the team's deliverable is the only thing that matters, not individual credit.\"\n\nTip: Align on the answer and ownership fast, communicate blockers early, prioritize decision-driving work, and help teammates — the team's output is what counts.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What do you do when you realize you'll miss a deadline?","Flag it immediately with options — re-scope, get help, or reset expectations — rather than going quiet and delivering a surprise failure at the deadline."]}],
["Ethics", "What do you do if a client clearly wants the analysis to support a predetermined conclusion?", "**Why they ask:** integrity under client pressure is a real test.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"My value is honest analysis, so I won't reverse-engineer evidence to a foregone conclusion — that's how clients make expensive mistakes and how advisors lose their worth. I'd do the real analysis, and if it supports their view, great. If it doesn't, I present the findings candidly and respectfully, make sure I understand why they favor that direction in case they know something I don't, and show the risks of the predetermined path. I can frame hard truths constructively, but I won't dress up a bad answer. If pushed to fabricate support, I'd decline and escalate internally rather than put my name on it.\"\n\nTip: Do the real analysis and present honest findings; understand their view, frame truth constructively — but never manufacture support for a predetermined answer.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you deliver a finding the client doesn't want to hear?","Respectfully and with evidence, acknowledging their perspective and the risks of ignoring the finding — honest framing, not a softened falsehood."]}],
["Learning", "How do you get up to speed on an unfamiliar industry quickly?", "**Why they ask:** strategists constantly work across new domains.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I attack it systematically. I read the fast overviews first — industry primers, analyst reports, the client's own materials — to build a mental map of how the industry makes money, who the players are, and what drives success and failure. Then I talk to people who know it: internal experts, and especially the client's frontline, who know where the bodies are buried. I learn the economics and the vocabulary fast so I can hold a credible conversation, while staying aware that a few weeks doesn't make me the expert — so I pair my structured outside view with their deep inside knowledge.\"\n\nTip: Build the money-and-players map from primers and reports, then learn from internal and frontline experts — combine your structured view with their depth.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's the most important thing to understand first about a new industry?","How it actually makes money and what drives winners versus losers — the profit engine and key success factors frame everything else."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a recommendation of yours that was rejected or didn't work out.", "**Why they ask:** humility, learning, and honesty about outcomes.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"I once recommended a client exit a underperforming product line. The analysis was sound on the numbers, but leadership rejected it, and in hindsight I'd underweighted how the line anchored key customer relationships that drove other sales — a factor I'd treated too lightly. The lesson was that a recommendation has to account for the whole system, not just the isolated P&L, and that I needed to engage leadership's concerns earlier rather than presenting a finished answer. I now pressure-test recommendations with the people who hold that context before finalizing.\"\n\nTip: Own a real case, name the analytical or stakeholder blind spot, and show the concrete change in how you work now.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's a common blind spot in a numbers-driven recommendation?","Ignoring system effects and relationships the isolated analysis doesn't capture — and not engaging the people who hold that context early enough."]}],
["Data", "How do you handle a project where the data is incomplete or unreliable?", "**Why they ask:** real engagements rarely have clean, complete data.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First I assess what I actually have and how trustworthy it is, so I don't build a confident answer on a shaky base. I triangulate — cross-check a number against a second independent source or a bottom-up estimate — and I'm explicit about which conclusions are solid and which are directional. Where the data gap is decision-critical, I'll invest to fill it with primary research or a targeted analysis; where it's not, I proceed with reasonable assumptions, clearly stated, and a sensitivity to show how much they matter. The goal is an honest, decision-useful answer with its uncertainty visible, not false precision.\"\n\nTip: Assess reliability, triangulate, fill decision-critical gaps, and state assumptions and uncertainty explicitly — honest and directional beats falsely precise.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why not just wait for perfect data?","Perfect data rarely comes, and decisions can't wait — the skill is reaching a sound, appropriately-caveated answer with the imperfect data you have."]}],
["Case", "How would you think about a company's operating model or organizational design?", "**Why they ask:** strategy often requires structure to follow.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Structure should follow strategy, so I start from what the business is trying to win at and ask whether the organization is set up to deliver it. I look at how work flows, where decisions are made, accountability, and whether the structure creates the right focus or buries key capabilities. Common issues are silos that block the cross-functional work the strategy needs, unclear ownership, or a structure optimized for a strategy the company has since moved past. I weigh trade-offs — a functional org drives depth and efficiency, a business-unit org drives market focus — and recommend the one that best serves the strategy, since there's no universally right answer.\"\n\nTip: Structure follows strategy — check whether decision rights, accountability, and work flow actually enable what the business is trying to win at.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is there no universally best org structure?","Each involves trade-offs — functional drives depth and efficiency, business-unit drives market focus — so the right one depends on what the strategy most needs."]}],
["Case", "How do you anticipate competitive response to a strategic move?", "**Why they ask:** strategy is interactive; ignoring rivals' reactions is naive.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I war-game it: for each major move we're considering, I ask how rational competitors would respond and whether our move still wins after they react. If we cut price to gain share and the obvious response is they match it, we may just destroy industry margins and end where we started — so the question is whether we have an advantage that makes the move stick, like a lower cost base they can't match. I look at competitors' incentives, capabilities, and history to judge how and how fast they'd react, and favor moves that are hard to copy or that rivals are unwilling or unable to match.\"\n\nTip: War-game rivals' likely responses and ask if the move still wins after they react — favor moves hard to copy or match.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why can a price cut to gain share backfire?","Competitors often match it, so share barely moves while everyone's margins fall — unless you have a cost advantage that lets you sustain it and they can't."]}],
["Reflection", "How do you measure whether a strategy project was successful?", "**Why they ask:** outcomes, not deliverables, define success.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Ultimately by the impact on the business — did the recommendation get implemented and move the metrics it was meant to move. In the near term, success is a clear, well-evidenced answer the client believes in and commits to acting on, with a realistic plan to execute. I'm wary of calling a project successful just because the deck was well-received; a brilliant analysis that changes nothing failed at the only thing that matters. So I care about adoption and the real-world result, and I try to stay connected enough to learn whether the strategy actually worked, not just whether the presentation landed.\"\n\nTip: Success is implementation and real business impact — not a well-received deck that changes nothing.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why isn't a well-received presentation enough?","Applause doesn't change the business — if the recommendation isn't implemented and doesn't move the metrics, the project failed at its actual purpose."]}],
["Analysis", "How do you avoid analysis paralysis and know when you have enough to recommend?", "**Why they ask:** strategists must act on sufficient, not perfect, information.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I tie the analysis to the decision: once I have enough to be confident in the recommendation and I've tested that more analysis wouldn't change it, I stop. I set that bar up front — what would I need to believe to recommend this, and what evidence would confirm or kill it — so I'm not endlessly gathering. If the remaining uncertainty wouldn't flip the decision, it's not worth resolving before recommending; I can flag it as something to monitor or test in a pilot. Decisiveness with appropriate confidence beats a perfect answer delivered too late to matter.\"\n\nTip: Stop when you're confident and more analysis wouldn't change the decision; monitor or pilot the uncertainty that remains rather than chasing perfection.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How do you set the bar for 'enough analysis' up front?","Define what you'd need to believe to recommend and what evidence confirms or kills it — then stop when that's met, not when uncertainty hits zero."]}],
["Closing", "What makes a great strategy or business analyst?", "**Why they ask:** self-awareness about the role's real skills.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Structured problem-solving to break a messy question into answerable parts; the analytical rigor to get the numbers right; and — the part people underrate — the judgment and communication to turn analysis into a decision people act on. A great analyst is also intellectually honest: willing to follow the evidence to an unwelcome answer and to say 'I don't know yet.' And they're genuinely curious about how businesses work. The technical skills get you in the door; the judgment, honesty, and ability to drive a decision are what make the analysis actually matter.\"\n\nTip: Structured problem-solving, analytical rigor, and the judgment and communication to turn analysis into action — plus intellectual honesty.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["Which of those is hardest to develop?","Judgment — knowing which analysis matters and how to turn it into a decision people act on — comes with experience, beyond the learnable technical craft."]}]
]);
